<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Drivers for safe working hours</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>A bus company needs \({@H@}\) hours of driving each week on its long-distance routes. Its safety rule is that no driver drives more than \({@h@}\) hours in a day, or on more than \({@d@}\) days in a week.</p><p>(a) What is the most one driver may drive in a week?</p><p>[[input:ans1]] hours [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) What is the least number of drivers the company needs?</p><p>[[input:ans2]] drivers [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p><p>(c) The company lowers the daily limit to \({@h2@}\) hours. What is the least number of drivers now?</p><p>[[input:ans3]] drivers [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) One driver may drive at most \({@h@} \times {@d@} = {@wk@}\) hours a week.</p><p>(b) \({@H@} \div {@wk@} = {@fr(q1)@} [[if test="is(integerp(10*q1))"]]= {@dd(q1)@}[[else]]\approx {@dd(r1(q1))@}[[/if]]\). Drivers come whole, so round up: \({@f1@}\) drivers give only \({@f1*wk@}\) hours, less than \({@H@}\), so the company needs \({@N1@}\) drivers.</p><p>(c) Now each driver may drive \({@h2@} \times {@d@} = {@wk2@}\) hours a week. \({@H@} \div {@wk2@} [[if test="is(integerp(10*q2))"]]= {@dd(q2)@}[[else]]\approx {@dd(r1(q2))@}[[/if]]\), so the company needs \({@N2@}\) drivers (\({@f2@}\) drivers give only \({@f2*wk2@}\) hours).</p><p>A lower limit means more drivers and a higher wage bill, but rested drivers are safer for passengers and for the drivers themselves.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
